      "edits": [
        {
          "new": "            if not JsonPointer._RE_ARRAY_INDEX.fullmatch(str(part)):\n                raise JsonPointerException(\"'%s' is not a valid sequence index\" % part)\n\n            try:\n                return int(part)\n            except ValueError:\n                raise JsonPointerException(\"'%s' is not a valid sequence index\" % part)",
          "old": "            if not JsonPointer._RE_ARRAY_INDEX.fullmatch(str(part)):\n                raise JsonPointerException(\"'%s' is not a valid sequence index\" % part)\n\n            return int(part)",
          "path": "jsonpointer.py"
        }
      ],
      "rationale": "The bug: `get_part` calls `int(part)` on a numeric string that passes the regex `_RE_ARRAY_INDEX`, but if the digit count exceeds Python's `sys.set_int_max_str_digits` limit (default 4300), `int()` raises `ValueError`, which leaks out of `resolve`, `to_last`, and `set`. Fix: wrap the `int(part)` conversion in a try/except ValueError and raise `JsonPointerException` instead. This preserves ordinary indices, end-of-list handling, and mutation behavior. Numeric object member keys are unaffected because `get_part` returns `part` unchanged for `Mapping` before reaching the sequence branch. New regression tests cover resolve, to_last, set, resolve-with-default, and long numeric mapping keys."
    }
